<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFrameworks>$(MauimeTargetFrameworks)</TargetFrameworks>
    <UseMauiCore>true</UseMauiCore>
    <SingleProject>true</SingleProject>
    <AssemblyName>Mauime.WebHostPatch</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>Mauime.WebHostPatch</RootNamespace>
    <PackageId>Mauime.WebHostPatch</PackageId>
    <Version>26.09.08</Version>
    <Product>Mauime.WebHostPatch</Product>
    <Description>Run an ASP.NET Core web host and Kestrel server inside a .NET MAUI app: start it, stop it, and find out what address to show the user.
Despite the name there is no patch in it. On .NET 10 a web host inside a MAUI app needs a framework reference and nothing else; the name is kept for continuity with Xamarinme.WebHostPatch.</Description>
    <PackageTags>maui kestrel aspnetcore webhost server</PackageTags>
    <PackageReleaseNotes>First release. Replaces Xamarinme.WebHostPatch 1.0.0.
That package was two forks of Microsoft's code, because ASP.NET Core 2.2 on Mono needed them. Console.CancelKeyPress threw, so ConsoleLifetime and WebHostExtensions.RunAsync were reimplemented around it; and Microsoft.Net.Http.Headers 2.2.0 called an InplaceStringBuilder that Microsoft.Extensions.Primitives 5.0 had deleted, so a fork of Primitives stamped 5.9.0.0 was shipped to outrank the real assembly at bind time. Neither cause survives on .NET 10 and neither fork came across: this package has no NuGet dependencies at all.
IMauimeWebHost starts and stops a WebApplication on Kestrel and reports the address it bound to, read from the server rather than from the options, so a port of 0 reports the port the operating system chose. Listening on every interface reports the machine's LAN address rather than 0.0.0.0.
NetworkAddress.GetLocalAddress replaces the Xamarinme demo's NetworkHelper, with its bug fixed: that version picked the first qualifying interface and only then filtered its addresses, so a machine whose first interface held only a link-local address reported none at all.
Worth knowing if you are upgrading: Xamarinme.WebHostPatch 1.0.0 pins ASP.NET Core 2.2.0, which carries a critical advisory in Microsoft.AspNetCore.Server.Kestrel.Core (GHSA-5rrx-jjjq-q2r5) and a moderate one in Microsoft.AspNetCore.Server.IIS (GHSA-prrf-397v-83xh). Its forked Primitives also shadows the real assembly for anything sharing an output directory.</PackageReleaseNotes>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      Microsoft.Maui.Core, not Microsoft.Maui.Controls: Core carries MauiAppBuilder, the
      LifecycleEvents builders and Microsoft.Maui.ApplicationModel, which is everything these
      libraries need. Microsoft.Maui.Essentials alone is not enough - it has none of them.
      Since .NET 8 the UseMaui* properties no longer add the package reference implicitly (MA002).
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Maui.Core" Version="$(MauiVersion)" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      No packages, and no fork of anything. On .NET 10 an ASP.NET Core web host inside a MAUI app
      needs a framework reference and nothing else - verified building WebApplication plus
      UseKestrel against net10.0-android. Xamarinme.WebHostPatch existed because ASP.NET Core 2.2
      on Mono needed both a patched ConsoleLifetime and a shadowing fork of
      Microsoft.Extensions.Primitives. Neither is needed here.
    -->
    <FrameworkReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.App" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
